    "done_when": "tests/test_a_live_worker_never_reads_gone_after_its_first_commit.py drives a stub worker in a temporary RECKON_HOME. The stub's recorded pid stays alive while one commit is made in its worktree beyond the run's base. The test then runs the live classifier and the producer's transition over that pointer. It asserts that no emitted transition reads \"the worker process is gone\" or classifies blocked with recovery resume while the pid is alive. It also asserts that the transition and the live classifier agree the worker is alive. At the node's base, the test is run and its result logged. If it passes at base, the manifest names the commit that removed the defect f-acpewio-first-commit-gone describes, with the evidence; 7bcd27ac0 is the candidate. In that case no product file changes. If it fails at base, the fix lands in reckon/crew/recovery.py, in the branches that write \"the worker process is gone with no recorded exit\" (around :4860 to :4875), so that the recorded pid's liveness decides, and the test passes. The declared negative control is executed and logged: in a scratch tree, make that classification treat the worker as dead whenever its worktree carries a commit, and the test fails. The test files that import reckon.crew.recovery are run at base and at head with pytest-xdist -n 8, each arm with its own --basetemp under /tmp, and they add no failing id. Every log sits under the run directory, headed with revision, tree and literal command, and ends EXIT=. Basetemps are deleted afterwards. Only the new test file and, if needed, reckon/crew/recovery.py change.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Prove by a test that a run whose recorded pid is alive never emits a process-gone transition after its first commit, repairing recovery.py only where that test fails at base",
    "id": "a-live-worker-never-reads-gone-after-its-first-commit",
